So that's the basic Wordle.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:12.340
One other thing that I really
like about the real game

00:00:12.340 --> 00:00:13.660
is that it shows a keyboard.

00:00:16.570 --> 00:00:19.750
It shows a keyboard of
what letters you've used

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:20.890
and which ones that match.

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:25.820
So we can add something
like that really quick.

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:28.250
So I'm just going
to keep it simple

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:31.430
and make another
text output like this

00:00:31.430 --> 00:00:36.740
and have that display the
keys that are remaining.

00:00:36.740 --> 00:00:43.050
So let's do another
verbatim text output.

00:00:45.635 --> 00:00:47.940
Call it keyboard.

00:00:47.940 --> 00:00:50.102
And also I just realized
one other thing,

00:00:50.102 --> 00:00:51.810
which is that we need
to give-- we should

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:54.570
give some indication what these
brackets and parentheses mean.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:57.672
So we can do that
by writing some--

00:00:57.672 --> 00:00:59.130
essentially just
writing some HTML.

00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:01.005
This is like a P HTML tag.

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:15.420
So let's just say bracket means
that the letter is correct--

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:19.375
yeah, letter is correct.

00:01:19.375 --> 00:01:22.430
I'll do that.

00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:24.910
And another one.

00:01:24.910 --> 00:01:29.960
So parentheses x means that
the letter is in the word

00:01:29.960 --> 00:01:32.090
but in the wrong place.

00:01:35.215 --> 00:01:35.715
OK.

00:01:40.890 --> 00:01:46.670
So that should-- yeah, so save.

00:01:46.670 --> 00:01:47.870
OK.

00:01:47.870 --> 00:01:51.620
So now we've added that
information there and now we

00:01:51.620 --> 00:01:52.805
can add a keyboard.

00:01:55.588 --> 00:01:56.880
Just for the remaining letters.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:01.300
So let's say output,
call it keyboard.

00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:02.500
Let's do render text again.

00:02:05.510 --> 00:02:08.150
And we need all the letters.

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:15.730
So i guess we'll just do
it the old fashioned way.

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:22.980
So letters equals.

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:27.030
And I think-- what's
the best way to do this?

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:28.335
Let's just do paste.

00:02:32.990 --> 00:02:39.970
Q. All right.

00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:44.960
So I'm making enough
space around these letters

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:50.663
to put a bracket around it
Oh, do we need to do that?

00:02:50.663 --> 00:02:52.080
I don't need to
do that right now,

00:02:52.080 --> 00:02:54.930
but I'm going to do it anyway.

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:57.330
And then this is going to be
shifted over a little bit.

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:08.060
All right.

00:03:08.060 --> 00:03:09.875
And one more.

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:17.530
OK.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:19.500
So those are our letters.

00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:20.380
That's our keyboard.

00:03:20.380 --> 00:03:26.920
And we're going to use a new
line between each of those.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:31.040
OK.

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:37.690
Actually, let me call this keys.

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:45.010
And so what I'm going to
do here is really basic.

00:03:45.010 --> 00:03:47.440
I'm just going to do
a search and replace.

00:03:47.440 --> 00:03:59.168
So what I need to do is take the
letters from all of the guesses

00:03:59.168 --> 00:04:03.915
and I need to check if each
one is in the keyboard.

00:04:13.740 --> 00:04:15.330
I'm doing this in a
really simple way.

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:18.089
I'm not going to
indicate on the keyboard

00:04:18.089 --> 00:04:22.200
which letters are correct or
that you used and were correct

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:25.080
and which ones were not
correct and not in the word.

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:27.343
That's how the real
Wordle game works.

00:04:27.343 --> 00:04:29.010
In this case, I'm
just going to indicate

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:34.672
which letters you've used up
in which ones are remaining.

00:04:34.672 --> 00:04:35.630
And that's really easy.

00:04:35.630 --> 00:04:39.130
So I can just take
all of the guesses.

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:43.880
I can say-- let's see.

00:04:43.880 --> 00:04:51.900
Used letters is-- first let's--

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:55.660
yeah.

00:04:55.660 --> 00:05:01.150
Let's paste together all of
the words and all guesses.

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:04.800
All guesses collapse
equals that.

00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:18.100
And then we can split that
into your character vector.

00:05:22.780 --> 00:05:25.960
Again, doing this string split
and then bracket one again

00:05:25.960 --> 00:05:28.720
like we did it before.

00:05:28.720 --> 00:05:34.090
And then we can say
used letters is unique.

00:05:36.640 --> 00:05:38.810
Put unique elements in that.

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:39.310
All right.

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:42.400
So let's just try
this at the console.

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:44.620
Let's just assign
something to all guesses.

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:48.075
Let's say this is first, second.

00:05:51.904 --> 00:05:54.043
Let's just say those
were our guesses.

00:05:54.043 --> 00:05:55.460
And then we can
step through here.

00:05:55.460 --> 00:06:02.940
So the used letters is those
two words stuck together.

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:07.090
Then we split it into
the individual letters.

00:06:09.623 --> 00:06:11.540
And then we take the
unique ones out of there.

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:13.530
So we should not
see, in this case,

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:17.870
there's two S's and we should
see one of those disappear.

00:06:21.560 --> 00:06:22.150
OK.

00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:27.280
So these are all the letters
that we've used so far.

00:06:27.280 --> 00:06:31.180
And we just need to remove
those from this keyboard.

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:32.092
We'll just use that.

00:06:32.092 --> 00:06:33.800
We'll do that using
a regular expression.

00:06:33.800 --> 00:06:34.630
So that should be--

00:06:34.630 --> 00:06:36.110
that's really easy.

00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:46.960
So let's say for letter in
used letter and used letters.

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:48.310
Actually let me just say letter.

00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:49.477
That's a little bit simpler.

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:56.000
Keys is.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:07:03.740
And we'll do-- we'll use
sub to replace letter.

00:07:03.740 --> 00:07:06.870
Oops, not in quotes.

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:09.770
Variable letter with
an empty string.

00:07:13.790 --> 00:07:16.050
And that's in keys.

00:07:21.460 --> 00:07:24.830
And at the end,
let's return keys.

00:07:24.830 --> 00:07:25.330
OK.

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:28.020
All right.

00:07:28.020 --> 00:07:29.740
Let's see if it works.

00:07:29.740 --> 00:07:30.615
There's our keyboard.

00:07:33.990 --> 00:07:35.400
And that's great.

00:07:35.400 --> 00:07:39.160
So let's say aisle.

00:07:39.160 --> 00:07:41.570
It did not do anything.

00:07:41.570 --> 00:07:44.470
So that-- [LAUGHS]
oh, you know why?

00:07:44.470 --> 00:07:46.130
OK, here's the problem.

00:07:46.130 --> 00:07:48.970
So this didn't work, and
that's because all guesses,

00:07:48.970 --> 00:07:55.120
this render text happened
once, but there's nothing

00:07:55.120 --> 00:07:58.083
because this is a
reactive object.

00:07:58.083 --> 00:08:00.250
And there's nothing that's
triggering the reactivity

00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:04.850
to re-execute because all guess
is just a regular variable.

00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:07.450
It's not a reactive one.

00:08:07.450 --> 00:08:09.640
So if we want
changes to this all

00:08:09.640 --> 00:08:16.730
guesses variable to trigger
this render text to change again

00:08:16.730 --> 00:08:20.370
or to re-execute and then
update which keys are available.

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:24.627
So there's sort of the
correct way of doing it

00:08:24.627 --> 00:08:26.210
and then there's the
sort of-- there's

00:08:26.210 --> 00:08:28.130
a hacky shortcut we could use.

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:30.410
And the hacky
shortcut we could use

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:35.150
is to have it listen
to this Go button.

00:08:35.150 --> 00:08:36.740
But sort of the
correct thing to do

00:08:36.740 --> 00:08:41.690
is to look for changes
to the all guesses vector

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:43.470
and re-execute
when that changes.

00:08:43.470 --> 00:08:47.990
So let's make all
guesses a reactive val.

00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:55.260
Reactive val which
has the starting value

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:58.260
of an empty character string.

00:08:58.260 --> 00:09:03.050
And we need to update it here.

00:09:09.218 --> 00:09:11.010
I'm going to split this
into two lines just

00:09:11.010 --> 00:09:13.030
to make it a little
clearer what's going on.

00:09:13.030 --> 00:09:21.090
So all guesses new is that.

00:09:21.090 --> 00:09:24.680
But because this
is reactive now,

00:09:24.680 --> 00:09:27.860
we need to invoke
it like a function

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:30.980
to get the existing
value out of it.

00:09:30.980 --> 00:09:34.980
We can't just access it
without the parentheses.

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:38.865
So we're going to take the
existing value for all guesses,

00:09:38.865 --> 00:09:40.490
append the new guess
onto it, and we're

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:42.407
going to save it in this
local variable called

00:09:42.407 --> 00:09:43.490
all guesses new.

00:09:43.490 --> 00:09:50.430
And then we need to assign
it back into all guesses.

00:09:50.430 --> 00:09:53.130
And the way we do
that is all guesses,

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:58.060
parentheses, all guesses new.

00:09:58.060 --> 00:10:00.190
That should assign it back.

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:05.110
And then when we
use it down here,

00:10:05.110 --> 00:10:07.220
we'll just invoke
it like a function.

00:10:07.220 --> 00:10:12.310
And because we're using this
reactive val from this render

00:10:12.310 --> 00:10:15.592
text, every time
this thing changes,

00:10:15.592 --> 00:10:17.800
the value here changes, it
will cause the render text

00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:18.670
to execute.

00:10:21.890 --> 00:10:23.480
So let's try this again.

00:10:23.480 --> 00:10:24.890
Aisle.

00:10:24.890 --> 00:10:26.690
Go.

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:28.550
That totally did not work.

00:10:28.550 --> 00:10:30.050
Error in compare words.

00:10:30.050 --> 00:10:31.140
Oh, yeah.

00:10:31.140 --> 00:10:31.640
Here we go.

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:38.680
We need to call a
function there as well.

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:39.180
All right.

00:10:39.180 --> 00:10:40.740
Reload.

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:43.180
Aisle.

00:10:43.180 --> 00:10:43.680
All right.

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:44.600
And then saves.

00:10:47.260 --> 00:10:47.800
Great.

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:49.960
As you can see, it's taken
the keys out of here.

00:10:53.820 --> 00:10:54.585
And gives.

00:10:57.100 --> 00:11:00.280
And that's working correctly.

00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:03.240
Now, there's one thing
I want to mention

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:08.980
about using the reactive val,
which is that one thing that's

00:11:08.980 --> 00:11:13.300
happening here when
we call all guesses

00:11:13.300 --> 00:11:18.720
is that normally when we
do this, because this is

00:11:18.720 --> 00:11:24.400
a reactive val, this
render text will take

00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:26.230
a dependency on all guesses.

00:11:26.230 --> 00:11:29.170
So whenever all guesses
changes, it would re-execute.

00:11:29.170 --> 00:11:34.915
So if you do this in many
cases, what can happen

00:11:34.915 --> 00:11:36.790
is you end up in this
sort of reactivity loop

00:11:36.790 --> 00:11:40.990
where you keep
appending something.

00:11:40.990 --> 00:11:43.720
You keep appending something
to all guesses and then

00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:45.970
that causes render
text to re-execute

00:11:45.970 --> 00:11:48.820
and then it grows it
again and it keeps

00:11:48.820 --> 00:11:51.010
going over and over again.

00:11:51.010 --> 00:11:55.240
We happen to not have that
problem here because we're

00:11:55.240 --> 00:11:57.820
using the bind event.

00:11:57.820 --> 00:12:00.520
So the only thing that
will cause this render

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:06.000
text to re-execute is when the
input Go button is pressed.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:11.620
And so when you do this bind
event on the render text

00:12:11.620 --> 00:12:14.650
or on a reactive or
observer, it makes it

00:12:14.650 --> 00:12:19.990
so that it doesn't listen
for changes to the reactive

00:12:19.990 --> 00:12:22.270
and validations of
things that are inside

00:12:22.270 --> 00:12:23.750
of this block of code.

00:12:23.750 --> 00:12:26.553
So we're lucky in
this case that we

00:12:26.553 --> 00:12:28.720
don't have to worry about
that sort of infinite loop

00:12:28.720 --> 00:12:30.880
happening.

00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:34.950
If we did, if we weren't
using the bind event

00:12:34.950 --> 00:12:37.590
and we're just using a render
text by itself the normal way,

00:12:37.590 --> 00:12:41.010
then we could use a
function called isolate.

00:12:41.010 --> 00:12:42.720
So we could say isolate.

00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:44.940
And then that would
cause this render text

00:12:44.940 --> 00:12:48.110
to not take a
reactive dependency

00:12:48.110 --> 00:12:49.070
on that reactive val.

00:12:49.070 --> 00:12:51.440
And we would have to
do it here and here.

00:12:54.290 --> 00:12:57.270
But we don't have to because
we're using bind event.

00:12:57.270 --> 00:12:58.880
So here we go.

00:12:58.880 --> 00:13:00.987
Yeah, this is our
basic Wordle game.

00:13:00.987 --> 00:13:02.570
I mean, this is the
basics of the game

00:13:02.570 --> 00:13:07.760
and you can waste a
lot of time playing it.

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:08.690
I already have.

00:13:08.690 --> 00:13:11.140
[LAUGHS]

